Reasoning: Pin the exact error class first — "Request has expired" and "ExpiredToken" implicate different clocks.
<|call:curl_presigned_url|> {"url": "https://media-vault-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/v/8817.mp4", "age_minutes": 92}
System Response: HTTP 403 — <Code>ExpiredToken</Code><Message>The provided token has expired.</Message>
Recovery reasoning: Could still be signing-host clock trouble; check skew and signer config.
<|call:check_ntp_skew|> {"host": "media-signer-prod-1"}
System Response: chrony offset +0.0004s, healthy; config audit: expires_seconds=604800 correctly applied to every URL issued since 16:00
Recovery reasoning: That green answers the wrong layer: a presigned URL is valid for min(X-Amz-Expires, signing-credential lifetime), and the error names the token — the credential, not the window. The signer moved to an instance role yesterday; decode what signs the URLs.
<|call:parse_presigned_url|> {"url": "https://media-vault-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/v/8817.mp4"}
System Response: X-Amz-Credential=ASIA3W... (ASIA prefix = temporary STS credentials); X-Amz-Security-Token present; instance-role session duration 3600s
Recovery reasoning: Every URL is signed by a 1-hour session token, so links die at session expiry (~65 min) regardless of the 7-day window — matching the timing and the 16:00 start. Point the signer at a long-term IAM user key in Secrets Manager.
<|call:deploy_signer_config|> {"service": "media-signer", "credential_source": "secretsmanager:svc-media-signer-key"}
System Response: rolled out 11:08 UTC; canary URL issued
<|call:curl_presigned_url|> {"url": "https://media-vault-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/v/canary.mp4", "decode": true}
System Response: HTTP 200; credential AKIA5Q... (long-term IAM user), no X-Amz-Security-Token parameter
Resolution: 604800s is exactly the SigV4 maximum, so 7-day links now hold. Root cause: presigned URLs inherited the instance role's 1h STS session lifetime; X-Amz-Expires never outlives the signing credentials.

